<title>INDIA: Indian soybean markets closed for Moslem festival.</title>
<headline>Indian soybean markets closed for Moslem festival.</headline>
<dateline>INDORE, India 1997-04-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Soybean markets in central India were closed on Friday for the Moslem festival of Eid al-Adha. Trading resumes on Saturday.</p>
<p>On Thursday soybean prices were firm on poor arrivals against good demand from processing plants. Prices rose 100-150 rupees per tonne and the market undertone was strong.</p>
<p>Soyoil prices were firm on limited supply from plants and refining units.</p>
<p>Soymeal and rapeseed extraction prices remained steady in very thin trading as buying and selling both were limited. Soymeal yellow was $318 per tonne FAS (Free Alongside Ship) in bulk for export. Rapeseed extraction was $118 per tonne FAS in bulk for export and was 4,100-4,150 rupees per tonne in FOR (Free on Rail) condition.</p>
